WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00.390 --> 00:04.271
So the first thing we’re going to start with is building your argument.

2
00:04.271 --> 00:09.190
Every argument needs a logical structure, and it needs to be airtight. Why does it matter?

3
00:09.510 --> 00:13.955
It matters because if you don’t have a logical, airtight structure, you’ll lose your audience.

4
00:13.955 --> 00:21.190
And the point of any communication, any argument in the communication, is to really have impact with your audience and drive them to some sort of action.

5
00:21.640 --> 00:27.550
An example of when I’ve seen structured communication go wrong is with an executive that we’ve been working with.

6
00:27.940 --> 00:31.940
It’s an industrial company that’s going through a huge transformation,

7
00:32.260 --> 00:38.690
so really large-scale change. And he really needs everybody in the organization to be working towards the same goal.

8
00:39.490 --> 00:48.030
His communication style is really fantastic. He has just really great energy in his message. He has a ton of conviction,

9
00:48.420 --> 00:55.550
and really motivates his audience. But behind those techniques, the message itself is not structured.

10
00:55.880 --> 00:59.999
It was not a logical grouping of ideas and supporting arguments.

11
00:59.999 --> 01:06.050
But if I had to describe his communication style, I’d say he was all charisma, but no content.

12
01:06.470 --> 01:11.940
And what happened was, he found out later everybody in his organization was working on different things.

13
01:12.450 --> 01:16.230
So he asked us to go talk to his teams to find out what happened.

14
01:16.550 --> 01:26.370
And when we went and we talked to everybody in the different organizations that report up into him, what we found is they all took away completely different things from his message.

15
01:26.730 --> 01:34.968
So the risk that you run when you don’t have a well-structured argument is that you don’t control the message and the takeaways that your audience has.

16
01:34.968 --> 01:45.280
After we spoke to his team and we learned that everybody had taken away such different messages, we actually went back to him and spent a significant amount of time structuring his message,

17
01:45.540 --> 01:52.130
structuring the arguments behind it, really building a much more coherent, logical argument,

18
01:52.450 --> 02:00.740
and he then repeated his whole communication cascade. But this time, it actually motivated people to start moving in the same direction.

19
02:01.190 --> 02:11.460
In fact, there are only negative consequences when you don’t have structure. The idea that you can be this rambling genius and

20
02:12.010 --> 02:16.350
still bring it all together — maybe you can, maybe if you’re just that smart, but

21
02:16.930 --> 02:20.450
frankly, I think those days are numbered. We have

22
02:20.770 --> 02:25.751
less room for complexity in our day-to-day lives, given how complex it already is.

23
02:25.751 --> 02:34.680
And I think what the pyramid principle, or broadly structuring and putting the emphasis, does is it takes naturally complex things that we all do —

24
02:34.980 --> 02:42.630
so the businesses we’re all in deal with quite difficult, highly interlinked concepts —

25
02:43.300 --> 02:49.540
and make it as straightforward as possible for someone to absorb it and, more importantly maybe, to replay it.

26
02:50.120 --> 02:58.340
Try and find a way to simplify, simplify, and simplify, and then further simplify the message that you’re giving.

27
02:58.760 --> 03:05.760
And I think that that’s one of the things that being a lawyer taught me: how to take an argument,

28
03:06.150 --> 03:13.350
or how to take some form of discourse and really distill it down to the core principles.

29
03:13.670 --> 03:22.370
And so if you can be found simple, generally people will understand what it is that you’re trying to convey.

30
03:23.080 --> 03:31.680
And so, you know, my observation is, in a world of investment banking, both on the capital markets side as well as on the banking and advisory side,

31
03:32.230 --> 03:40.390
there’s room for all of us to do more of this. So, whether you’re the trader having the morning call with your

32
03:41.000 --> 03:47.460
trading group, looking at the macroeconomic conditions, how they’ve changed overnight, and getting to

33
03:48.420 --> 03:53.950
the broad changes that have happened across your asset class, or affecting your asset class, and then

34
03:54.370 --> 03:58.750
going deep into the couple that are going to change the market pricing for that day;

35
03:59.490 --> 04:09.350
whether you’re the sales guy getting on the phone — you have, like, a couple of minutes to make the pitch to your counterparty, and

36
04:09.990 --> 04:13.510
you need to make it super simple, because you know what?

37
04:14.340 --> 04:20.388
He or she on the other side can’t make the call by themselves, and they’re going to have to go back to others.

38
04:20.388 --> 04:28.250
So how do you make it easy for them to understand, but more importantly, make it easy for them to go back and get the approvals that they need?

39
04:28.360 --> 04:38.150
Whether you’re the banker getting on that quick follow-up call after a pitch, trying to reinforce a few messages,

40
04:38.530 --> 04:50.310
and being able to put it in a way that really makes it simple, that takes that 80-page pitch document and distills it once again down to a few clear thoughts.

41
04:50.950 --> 04:58.660
You know, there are so many occasions where these things matter. And my personal feeling is that

42
04:59.050 --> 05:06.660
it’s always worth the slightly incremental investment to get that right before you get on that phone, before you take that meeting.

43
05:07.140 --> 05:13.630
And in fact, even if it shortens the amount of time you get with the other person, that trade-off is always worth it.

